### 2.10 Resolving a run (`runDir(id)`, `reportPath(id)`)

1. `id` must be a valid name (2.2), else `invalid run id "<id>"`.
2. Records directory `""` -> `resolve record directory: run inside a Git
   repository`.
3. List the records directory; failure (including "does not exist") ->
   `no run artifacts in <dir>: <err>`.
4. Matches = directory entries (real directories, not symlinks) whose
   name starts with `id`, sorted bytewise.
5. 0 matches -> `no run matches "<id>" under <dir>`; 1 match -> its path;
   more -> `"<id>" is ambiguous: matches <a>, <b>, ...` (comma-space
   joined, sorted).

`reportPath(id)` = `<runDir(id)>/report.md`. A full run ID or any unique
prefix resolves.

All of these are plain errors (exit 4 from `runs show`), not usage errors.

### 2.11 Shared serialization rules

- **String escaping.** Go's `encoding/json` escapes `<`, `>`, `&` as
  `<`, `>`, `&`, and U+2028 / U+2029 as ` ` /
  ` `, and replaces invalid UTF-8 with U+FFFD. To keep files
  byte-close to Go, every JSON file in this area is produced by a
  `goStringify(value, indent?)` helper: `JSON.stringify` then replace each
  of those five characters in the output with its `\uXXXX` form (safe
  because they can only occur inside JSON strings). Parity requirement is
  parse-equivalence; byte equality is the goal.
- **Raw JSON compaction** (`compactJSON(text)`) for provider bodies:
  a single-pass lexer that drops whitespace (space, tab, CR, LF) outside
  strings and applies the same five-character escaping inside strings,
  leaving everything else byte-for-byte.
- **Numbers.** Integers serialize as integers. `confidence_floor` and
  finding confidences are JS numbers (`JSON.stringify` output matches Go
  for the values Roast produces).
- **Timestamps.**
  - `meta.ts`, `findings.jsonl` `ts`: UTC, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS[.frac]Z`,
    where `frac` is the sub-second part with trailing zeros removed and
    the dot omitted when zero (Go `RFC3339Nano`). JS has millisecond
    precision, so at most 3 fraction digits.
  - index `ts`: local time (Go parity, kept; `meta.ts` stays UTC),
    `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS` followed by `Z` if the local UTC offset is zero, else
    `+HH:MM` / `-HH:MM` (Go `RFC3339`).
- **Bytewise sort**: compare names as UTF-8 byte strings
  (`Buffer.compare`), not with JS default `sort()`.

Checksum: the unsigned sum of all 512 header bytes with the chksum field
taken as eight spaces (0x20), written as `%06o` + NUL + space. Verified
against Go 1.26: `meta.json`, 9 bytes -> chksum `011006\0 `,
size `00000000011\0`.

PAX path records. Go switches to a PAX header for a name that does not
fit ustar: longer than 100 bytes with no `/` split that fits the
`prefix` field (run files are flat, so there is never a `/`), or
containing non-ASCII bytes. Roast's own artifact names never trigger
this, but a user can drop such a file into a run directory, and the build
must still succeed. The TS writer emits, immediately before the file's
own header block:

1. A PAX extended header entry: a 512-byte header with typeflag `x`
   (0x78), name `<dir>/PaxHeaders.0/<base>` (Go's `path.Join(dir,
   "PaxHeaders.0", base)`; for flat run files `<dir>` is empty, giving
   `PaxHeaders.0/<base>`), ASCII-only and truncated to 100 bytes, mode
   `0000000\0`, uid/gid/mtime zero, size = byte length of the records
   block (octal, as in every size field), magic `ustar\0` version `00`,
   then the records block zero-padded to a multiple of 512. Verified
   against Go 1.26 `archive/tar` on 2026-11-18 (long-name case).
2. The records block: one record, `<len> path=<name>\n`, where `<name>`
   is the full UTF-8 name and `<len>` is the decimal byte length of the
   whole record including the length digits and the newline (compute by
   fixed-point: start from `digits(len(rest)) + len(rest)` and adjust
   until stable).
3. The file's own header as in the table, with the name field holding
   the name truncated to 100 bytes (non-ASCII bytes dropped, matching
   Go's ASCII fallback), then the data and padding as usual.

Match Go's `archive/tar` for these headers as closely as practical; a
fixture captured from Go for one long name and one non-ASCII name is the
reference, and any divergence is documented next to the fixture. The
reader accepts the result regardless (below). A size of 8 GiB or more
(Go would add a PAX `size` record) still throws: the archive is built in
memory and such a file cannot be read.

#### Compression

- Go uses `klauspost/compress/zstd` with default options (single frame,
  default speed level, content checksum on). The TS port uses
  `zlib.zstdCompressSync(tar, { params: {
  [ZSTD_c_compressionLevel]: 3, [ZSTD_c_checksumFlag]: 1 } })`.
- Node 22.15+ marks `zlib.zstd*` experimental, and first use may print an
  `ExperimentalWarning` to stderr. Suppress that one warning only (spec.md
  "Runtime"), not warnings in general. The same applies to decompression
  in `verifyBundle`.
- The compressed bytes will differ from Go's (spec.md "Out of Scope"), so
  `bundle.tar.zst` digests differ between implementations. Required: (a) the
  decompressed tar is byte-identical to Go's for identical run files,
  (b) the TS output is deterministic: same run files -> same bundle
  bytes regardless of run directory path, file mtimes, owner, or file
  modes, (c) TS can verify bundles written by Go and vice versa.

### 2.13 Verify (`verifyBundle(path)`)

Validates format version, flat regular-file layout, and every manifest
size and digest; returns the manifest.

1. Open the file (raw error returned). Decompress zstd (error
   `open zstd stream: <err>` or, for a corrupt stream, `read tar: <err>`).
2. Read tar entries until the end-of-archive marker. Header or framing
   errors -> `read tar: <err>`. The reader must validate the header
   checksum (accept either the unsigned or signed byte sum, as Go does)
   and, when the ustar magic is present and `prefix` is non-empty, use
   `prefix + "/" + name` as the entry name. Supported header types are
   ustar plus PAX: an `x` entry (and a global `g` entry, whose records
   are ignored apart from consuming its data) is parsed as `<len> key=value`
   records, and a `path` record overrides the name of the next entry.
   A record with a bad length, a missing `=` or newline, or a length
   past the block is a `read tar: <err>` error. GNU long-name (`L`, `K`)
   headers are not required: they are rejected as non-regular entries
   by the typeflag check below.
3. For each entry:
   - typeflag must be `0` or NUL (legacy regular file), else
     `bundle entry "<name>" is not a regular file`;
   - name must be a valid name (2.2), else
     `bundle entry "<name>" is not a flat path`;
   - a name already seen, or a second manifest entry ->
     `bundle contains duplicate entry "<name>"`;
   - the manifest entry: header size over 1 MiB ->
     `bundle manifest is larger than 1048576 bytes`; otherwise read it
     (error `read bundle manifest: <err>`);
   - any other entry: SHA-256 and byte count of its data (error
     `read bundle entry <name>: <err>`).
4. No manifest -> `bundle has no bundle-manifest.json`.
5. Parse the manifest strictly: keys match exactly (case-sensitive, so
   `"Format"` is an unknown key; deliberate change 7), unknown keys
   rejected, exactly one JSON value (trailing data ->
   `parse bundle manifest: trailing JSON data`), types `format:string`,
   `version:integer`, `files:array|null` of
   `{path:string, sha256:string, size:integer}`. Errors
   `parse bundle manifest: <err>`.
6. `format != "roast-run-bundle"` -> `unsupported bundle format "<f>"`.
   `version != 1` -> `unsupported bundle version <v>`.
7. `files.length !=` number of non-manifest entries ->
   `bundle manifest lists <m> files but archive contains <n>`.
8. For each manifest entry in order:
   - path empty, not a basename, or equal to `bundle-manifest.json` ->
     `bundle manifest contains invalid path "<p>"`;
   - not strictly greater (bytewise) than the previous path ->
     `bundle manifest paths are not strictly sorted`;
   - `sha256` not 64 lowercase hex chars ->
     `bundle manifest has invalid sha256 for <p>`;
   - not in the archive -> `bundle manifest entry <p> is missing from
     archive`;
   - size mismatch -> `bundle entry <p> size is <actual>, manifest says
     <expected>`;
   - digest mismatch -> `bundle entry <p> digest does not match
     manifest`.
9. Return the manifest.

Archive entry order is not checked; only the manifest's path order is.

### 2.14 `roast runs`

```
roast runs [--limit N] [--json]
```

- No positional arguments; any argument (including unknown subcommands
  such as `runs sync` or `runs status`) is a usage error, exit 2.
- `--limit` integer, default `20`. `<= 0` means no limit.
- `--json` boolean, default false.
- Subcommands: `show`, `checkout`, `prune` (the last two: snapshot area).
package cli

import (
	"bufio"
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"os"
	"path/filepath"
	"sort"
	"time"

	"github.com/spf13/cobra"

	"github.com/bluesky-social/roast/internal/record"
	"github.com/bluesky-social/roast/internal/scope"
	"github.com/bluesky-social/roast/internal/snapshot"
	"github.com/bluesky-social/roast/internal/textsafe"
)

func newRunsCommand(stdout, stderr io.Writer) *cobra.Command {
	var limit int
	var rawJSON bool
	cmd := &cobra.Command{
		Use: "runs", Short: "Show and inspect local review runs", Args: noArgs,
		SilenceUsage: true, SilenceErrors: true,
		RunE: func(_ *cobra.Command, _ []string) error {
			dir := record.DefaultDir()
			if dir == "" {
				return &usageError{msg: "cannot resolve record directory: " +
					"run inside a Git repository"}
			}
			files, _ := filepath.Glob(filepath.Join(dir, "runs-*.jsonl"))
			sort.Strings(files)
			lines := tailRunLines(files, limit)
			if len(lines) == 0 {
				fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "roast: no run records in %s\n", dir)
				return nil
			}
			for _, line := range lines {
				if rawJSON {
					fmt.Fprintln(stdout, line)
					continue
				}
				var run struct {
					TS         string   `json:"ts"`
					RunID      string   `json:"run_id"`
					Repo       string   `json:"repo"`
					Mode       string   `json:"mode"`
					Assessment string   `json:"assessment"`
					Quick      bool     `json:"quick"`
					NFindings  int      `json:"n_findings"`
					NRefuted   int      `json:"n_refuted"`
					Files      []string `json:"files_reviewed"`
				}
				if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(line), &run); err != nil {
					continue
				}
				fmt.Fprintf(stdout, "%s  %-25s %-8s q=%-5v %-30s findings=%d refuted=%d files=%d  %s\n",
					run.TS, run.RunID, run.Mode, run.Quick, run.Assessment,
					run.NFindings, run.NRefuted, len(run.Files), run.Repo)
			}
			return nil
		},
	}
	cmd.Flags().IntVar(&limit, "limit", 20, "maximum runs to show")
	cmd.Flags().BoolVar(&rawJSON, "json", false, "emit raw JSONL records")
	cmd.AddCommand(
		newRunsShowCommand(stdout), newRunsCheckoutCommand(stdout, stderr),
		newRunsPruneCommand(stdout, stderr),
	)
	return cmd
}

func tailRunLines(files []string, limit int) []string {
	var lines []string
	for _, path := range files {
		file, err := os.Open(path)
		if err != nil {
			continue
		}
		scanner := bufio.NewScanner(file)
		scanner.Buffer(make([]byte, 64*1024), 16*1024*1024)
		for scanner.Scan() {
			lines = append(lines, scanner.Text())
		}
		_ = file.Close()
	}
	if limit > 0 && len(lines) > limit {
		lines = lines[len(lines)-limit:]
	}
	return lines
}

func newRunsShowCommand(stdout io.Writer) *cobra.Command {
	return &cobra.Command{
		Use: "show <run-id>", Short: "Print a run's Markdown report", Args: usageArgs(cobra.ExactArgs(1)),
		SilenceUsage: true, SilenceErrors: true,
		RunE: func(_ *cobra.Command, args []string) error {
			path, err := record.ReportPath(args[0])
			if err != nil {
				return err
			}
			data, err := os.ReadFile(path)
			if err != nil {
				return fmt.Errorf("run has no report artifact: %w", err)
			}
			// Reports written before render escaped finding text can hold raw
			// control bytes from model output; escape on the way out too.
			_, err = io.WriteString(stdout, textsafe.Text(string(data)))
			return err
		},
	}
}

func newRunsCheckoutCommand(stdout, stderr io.Writer) *cobra.Command {
	return &cobra.Command{
		Use: "checkout <run-id> [dir]", Short: "Materialize a run snapshot into a linked worktree",
		Args: usageArgs(cobra.RangeArgs(1, 2)), SilenceUsage: true, SilenceErrors: true,
		RunE: func(_ *cobra.Command, args []string) error {
			meta, _, err := record.ReadMeta(args[0])
			if err != nil {
				return &usageError{msg: err.Error()}
			}
			if !meta.Snapshot.Captured {
				return &usageError{msg: fmt.Sprintf("run %s has no Git snapshot (%s)", meta.RunID, meta.Snapshot.Reason)}
			}
			dest := ""
			if len(args) == 2 {
				dest = args[1]
			} else {
				base, err := os.MkdirTemp("", "roast-checkout-")
				if err != nil {
					return err
				}
				dest = filepath.Join(base, meta.RunID)
			}
			if _, err := snapshot.Restore(meta.Repo, meta.RunID, dest); err != nil {
				return fmt.Errorf("restore run %s: %w", meta.RunID, err)
			}
			fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "roast: remove with: git -C %s worktree remove --force %s\n", meta.Repo, dest)
			fmt.Fprintln(stdout, dest)
			return nil
		},
	}
}

func newRunsPruneCommand(stdout, stderr io.Writer) *cobra.Command {
	var olderThan time.Duration
	var dryRun bool
	cmd := &cobra.Command{
		Use: "prune", Short: "Delete aged Roast snapshot refs from the current repository", Args: noArgs,
		SilenceUsage: true, SilenceErrors: true,
		RunE: func(_ *cobra.Command, _ []string) error {
			repo, err := scope.GitRoot(".")
			if err != nil {
				return &usageError{msg: err.Error()}
			}
			items, err := snapshot.Prune(repo, olderThan, dryRun)
			if err != nil {
				return err
			}
			if len(items) == 0 {
				fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "roast: no snapshot refs older than %s\n", olderThan)
			}
			for _, item := range items {
				verb := "deleted"
				if dryRun {
					verb = "would delete"
				}
				fmt.Fprintf(stdout, "%s %s (age %s)\n", verb, item.Ref, item.Age.Round(time.Hour))
			}
			return nil
		},
	}
	cmd.Flags().DurationVar(&olderThan, "older-than", snapshot.DefaultRefRetention, "delete refs older than this")
	cmd.Flags().BoolVar(&dryRun, "dry-run", false, "list refs without deleting them")
	return cmd
}
// How the CLI finds and loads the project config (spec 02, 2.2). Every
// command loads it through loadProjectConfig; failures are usage errors.
import { gitRoot } from '../git/git.js'
import { openCredentialStore } from '../credentials/credentials.js'
import {
    type ProjectConfig,
    findConfig,
    loadConfig,
    withStoredCredentials,
} from '../config/load.js'
import type { RunContext } from './command.js'
import { UsageError } from './errors.js'

export const NO_PROJECT_CONFIG = 'no project config (.roast.json) found ' +
    'between here and the Git toplevel; run `npx roast init` to create one'

export const NO_GATE_CONFIG = 'the pre-commit gate runs from the Git ' +
    'toplevel and finds no .roast.json there; put the project config at ' +
    'the toplevel (`npx roast init`) before enabling the gate'

type ConfigContext = Pick<RunContext, 'cwd'|'env'|'signal'>

function usage (err:unknown):UsageError {
    const message = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err)
    return new UsageError(message, { cause:err })
}

// The config path: `explicit` (--config) unchanged when set, else the
// nearest .roast.json from the working directory up to the Git toplevel
// (the working directory itself outside a repository). '' when none.
export async function projectConfigPath (
    explicit:string,
    ctx:ConfigContext
):Promise<string> {
    if (explicit !== '') return explicit
    let stop = ctx.cwd
    try {
        stop = await gitRoot(ctx.cwd, {
            env:ctx.env as NodeJS.ProcessEnv,
            signal:ctx.signal,
        })
    } catch (err) {
        if (ctx.signal.aborted) throw err
    }
    return findConfig(ctx.cwd, stop)
}

// The config the pre-commit hook sees: only <toplevel>/.roast.json.
export function gateConfigPath (repo:string):string {
    return findConfig(repo, repo)
}

export async function loadProjectConfig (
    explicit:string,
    ctx:ConfigContext
):Promise<ProjectConfig> {
    let file:string
    try {
        file = await projectConfigPath(explicit, ctx)
    } catch (err) {
        if (ctx.signal.aborted) throw err
        throw usage(err)
    }
    if (file === '') throw new UsageError(NO_PROJECT_CONFIG)
    let cfg:ProjectConfig
    try {
        cfg = loadConfig(file)
    } catch (err) {
        throw usage(err)
    }
    // Every loaded config carries the stored-credentials store for the
    // working directory; roles read its files only when they reach the
    // stored step.
    const stored = await openCredentialStore(ctx.cwd, ctx.env,
        { signal:ctx.signal })
    return withStoredCredentials(cfg, stored)
}
// Neutralizes terminal control sequences in untrusted text before it reaches
// a terminal or a Markdown report (Go: internal/textsafe).
//
// `text` keeps line structure (LF and tab survive, CRLF becomes LF);
// `line` keeps everything on one line (LF and tab become `\n` and `\t`).
// Controls, DEL, C1 controls, invalid UTF-8 bytes, and lone surrogates
// become printable escapes. Backslash is never escaped, so both functions
// are idempotent.

// A decoded unit: a code point (>= 0), or an invalid byte stored as
// -(byte + 1).
type Unit = number

// Fast path: true when the mode would change nothing in `s`. A
// well-formed surrogate pair is clean; a lone surrogate is not.
function isClean (s:string, lineMode:boolean):boolean {
    for (let i = 0; i < s.length; i++) {
        const c = s.charCodeAt(i)
        if (c < 0x20) {
            if (lineMode || (c !== 0x0a && c !== 0x09)) return false
        } else if (c >= 0x7f && c <= 0x9f) {
            return false
        } else if (c >= 0xd800 && c <= 0xdbff) {
            const next = s.charCodeAt(i + 1)
            if (!(next >= 0xdc00 && next <= 0xdfff)) return false
            i++
        } else if (c >= 0xdc00 && c <= 0xdfff) {
            return false
        }
    }
    return true
}

function hex (n:number, width:number):string {
    return n.toString(16).padStart(width, '0')
}

function escapeUnits (units:Unit[], lineMode:boolean):string {
    let out = ''
    for (let i = 0; i < units.length; i++) {
        const u = units[i]
        if (u < 0) {
            out += '\\x' + hex(-u - 1, 2)
        } else if (u === 0x0d) {
            if (!lineMode && units[i + 1] === 0x0a) continue
            out += '\\x0d'
        } else if (u === 0x0a) {
            out += lineMode ? '\\n' : '\n'
        } else if (u === 0x09) {
            out += lineMode ? '\\t' : '\t'
        } else if (u < 0x20 || u === 0x7f) {
            out += '\\x' + hex(u, 2)
        } else if ((u >= 0x80 && u <= 0x9f) ||
            (u >= 0xd800 && u <= 0xdfff)) {
            out += '\\u' + hex(u, 4)
        } else {
            out += String.fromCodePoint(u)
        }
    }
    return out
}

function stringUnits (s:string):Unit[] {
    const units:Unit[] = []
    // String iteration yields a lone surrogate as its own code point.
    for (const ch of s) units.push(ch.codePointAt(0)!)
    return units
}

function isCont (b:number|undefined):boolean {
    return b !== undefined && b >= 0x80 && b <= 0xbf
}

// Decodes one rune at `i` with Go utf8.DecodeRune validity rules. Returns
// [codePoint, size], or [-1, 1] for an invalid or truncated sequence.
function decodeRune (b:Uint8Array, i:number):[number, number] {
    const b0 = b[i]
    if (b0 < 0x80) return [b0, 1]
    const b1 = b[i + 1]
    if (b0 >= 0xc2 && b0 <= 0xdf) {
        if (!isCont(b1)) return [-1, 1]
        return [((b0 & 0x1f) << 6) | (b1 & 0x3f), 2]
    }
    if (b0 >= 0xe0 && b0 <= 0xef) {
        const lo = b0 === 0xe0 ? 0xa0 : 0x80
        const hi = b0 === 0xed ? 0x9f : 0xbf
        if (b1 === undefined || b1 < lo || b1 > hi) return [-1, 1]
        const b2 = b[i + 2]
        if (!isCont(b2)) return [-1, 1]
        return [((b0 & 0x0f) << 12) | ((b1 & 0x3f) << 6) | (b2 & 0x3f), 3]
    }
    if (b0 >= 0xf0 && b0 <= 0xf4) {
        const lo = b0 === 0xf0 ? 0x90 : 0x80
        const hi = b0 === 0xf4 ? 0x8f : 0xbf
        if (b1 === undefined || b1 < lo || b1 > hi) return [-1, 1]
        const b2 = b[i + 2]
        const b3 = b[i + 3]
        if (!isCont(b2) || !isCont(b3)) return [-1, 1]
        return [((b0 & 0x07) << 18) | ((b1 & 0x3f) << 12) |
            ((b2 & 0x3f) << 6) | (b3 & 0x3f), 4]
    }
    return [-1, 1]
}

function byteUnits (b:Uint8Array):Unit[] {
    const units:Unit[] = []
    for (let i = 0; i < b.length;) {
        const [cp, size] = decodeRune(b, i)
        units.push(cp < 0 ? -(b[i] + 1) : cp)
        i += size
    }
    return units
}

// Multi-line sanitizing of a string.
export function text (s:string):string {
    if (isClean(s, false)) return s
    return escapeUnits(stringUnits(s), false)
}

// Single-line sanitizing of a string.
export function line (s:string):string {
    if (isClean(s, true)) return s
    return escapeUnits(stringUnits(s), true)
}

// Multi-line sanitizing of raw bytes, escaping each invalid UTF-8 byte.
export function textBytes (b:Uint8Array):string {
    return escapeUnits(byteUnits(b), false)
}

// Single-line sanitizing of raw bytes.
export function lineBytes (b:Uint8Array):string {
    return escapeUnits(byteUnits(b), true)
}
